# Vue pages & components

- Always Composition API + `<script setup lang="ts">` + TypeScript.
- Keep business logic out of UI when possible; one responsibility per component.
- Reuse shells: `Loading`, `NotificationMessage`.
- Form controls: `components/inputs/` (`InputText`).
- Never call `fetch` or build HTTP/transform API payloads here — use `@/api/*` and transformers.

## Input error contract

Inputs never read errors from a store; the page passes them down as `errors: string[]`
(see `InputText`), rendering `errors[0]`. New inputs follow the same shape.

Error state adds `form-input-error` to the base class and renders the message in `.form-help-error`.

## Props & emits

```ts
interface Props {
  user: User
  disabled?: boolean
}

const props = defineProps<Props>()

const emit = defineEmits<{
  save: [user: User]
  cancel: []
}>()
```

`withDefaults()` when optional props need defaults. Avoid runtime prop declarations unless required.

## State & templates

- Prefer `computed()` over watchers; `watch()` only for side effects.
- `ref()` for primitives/single values; `reactive()` for multi-field forms.
- Keep templates declarative; move non-trivial expressions to `computed()` or functions.
- Component classes from `main.css` for recurring patterns.
  Inline styles only when dynamic (e.g. a color swatch).

## Loading

```ts
const loading = ref(false)

try {
  loading.value = true
  // ...
} finally {
  loading.value = false
}
```
